d": 4.39, "poll_weight": 0.57, "fundamentals": {"margin": 7.46, "lean": 0.0, "environment": 7.47, "incumbency": 0.0, "money": -0.01}, "rating_margin": 3.65, "sigma_race": 5.5, "sigma_total": 6.15, "baseline_margin": 4.17, "analyst_adjustment": -1.17, "key_factors": ["October polls consistently show El-Sayed ahead by one to six points, averaging about three", "Prediction markets price El-Sayed near 69% to win", "El-Sayed's large fundraising edge is partly offset by heavy Republican outside spending against him", "Open seat with expert ratings split between Tossup and Lean D"], "rationale": "Polling averages, expert ratings, and betting markets all point to a modest El-Sayed edge in a state with no partisan lean, and his more-than-double fundraising haul reinforces that lead. Heavy outside advertising against him and a contest that has narrowed in recent weeks keep the margin too small for anything beyond Lean D.", "watch": "A late wave of Republican outside spending or further shifts among voters focused on the Gaza war could pull the race into a true tossup.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Gary Peters (D) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Michigan's open Senate seat pits Democrat Abdul El-Sayed against Republican Mike Rogers after Gary Peters chose to retire. October polling consistently shows El-Sayed slightly ahead, with most surveys in the one-to-six-point range and an average near three. Expert ratings lean Democratic, though several outlets call the race a tossup, and prediction markets give El-Sayed roughly a two-in-three chance.\n\nEl-Sayed has raised more than twice Rogers' total, but Republican outside groups have spent heavily against him and the contest has narrowed in recent weeks. The Gaza war has complicated his standing with some voters, while Trump's attacks and a first debate have kept the race competitive. Michigan's even partisan balance makes a small Democratic edge fragile, and turnout in the final stretch will likely decide